course: biology
lesson: 382
title: The Chinese zodiac: the twelve real animals behind the signs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: On a Chinese zodiac poster there are twelve animals: rat, ox, tiger, rabbit, dragon, snake, horse, goat, monkey, rooster, dog and pig. Each year belongs to one of them, so your animal comes back every twelve years.
explain: Eleven of the twelve are real animals, and only the dragon is made up, though it looks like a mix of several real ones.
explain: The old stories are legends: the rat did not really win a race by riding on the ox's back. But behind each sign is a real animal with its own true story.
explain: Water buffaloes pulled ploughs through flooded rice fields, wild horses were saved by zoos, and chickens and pigs were tamed in Asia thousands of years ago.
explain: So the zodiac is a good way to learn real biology: enjoy the legend, then ask what the real animal is like.
fact: The twelve zodiac animals are rat, ox, tiger, rabbit, dragon, snake, horse, goat, monkey, rooster, dog and pig; eleven are real and one, the dragon, is not.
fact: Each year belongs to one animal, so the same animal comes back every twelve years.
fact: Real rats eat almost anything, have babies quickly and learn fast, and they spread round the world on people's ships and carts.
fact: Water buffaloes pulled ploughs through wet rice fields in southern China for thousands of years; their wide hooves stop them sinking deep in mud.
fact: Water buffaloes have few sweat glands, so they cool down by lying in water and mud.
fact: People in China say the marks on a tiger's forehead look like the character for king; the stripes are really camouflage, and the South China tiger now survives only in zoos and reserves.
fact: The dark patches on the Moon are plains of old cooled lava, and people in China, Japan and Korea see a rabbit there, because our brains love finding faces and animal shapes in patterns; China named its Moon rovers Yutu, the jade rabbit.
fact: Przewalski's horses, the last truly wild horses, died out in the wild and were saved by zoos, then brought back to Mongolia and to Xinjiang in China.
fact: The Chinese character for the eighth animal can mean goat, sheep or ram, so both are right.
fact: The Monkey King is a story hero, but rhesus macaques really live wild in China and quickly learn to grab food and phones from tourists.
fact: Chickens come from the red junglefowl of southern China and South-East Asia, and pigs were tamed in China about 9,000 years ago.
fact: Dogs were the first animals people tamed, from wolves that were less afraid of people, but where and when is still argued.
exercise: How many animals are in the Chinese zodiac? | Twelve | Only ten | Just seven | Thirteen
exercise: Which zodiac animal is not a real animal? | The dragon | The strong ox | The rooster | The fluffy rabbit
exercise: How often does the same zodiac animal come back? | Every twelve years | Once every single year | Every ten years or so | Every hundred years
exercise: Why do real rats do so well in cities? | They eat almost anything, breed fast and learn quickly | They are protected by law everywhere, so nobody is allowed to catch them | They only eat fresh fruit | They sleep all winter
exercise: How did rats spread round the world? | On people's ships and carts | By swimming across every ocean | By flying along with flocks of birds | They were always everywhere already
exercise: What work did water buffaloes do in southern China? | Pulled ploughs through flooded rice fields | Carried letters and parcels between towns in the hills | Guarded houses at night | Pulled boats out at sea
exercise: Why does a buffalo not sink deep into mud? | Its wide hooves spread its weight | It walks on its knees | It is very light for its size, like a big balloon | It floats like a duck
exercise: How does a water buffalo cool down? | By lying in water and mud | By sweating a lot | By panting like a dog | By flapping its ears like an elephant
exercise: What are a tiger's stripes really for? | Camouflage in grass and shadows | Showing the other animals that it is king | Keeping it warm in snow | Scaring away flies
exercise: What are the dark patches on the Moon? | Huge plains of old cooled lava | Real oceans full of salty water | Forests and jungles seen from very far away | Shadows of the Earth
exercise: Why do we see shapes like a rabbit in the Moon? | Our brains find faces and animal shapes in patterns | Someone drew a rabbit there | A real rabbit lives there | The Moon changes its shape each night, and some nights it looks like one
exercise: How were Przewalski's horses saved? | Zoo horses were bred and brought back to the wild | They hid in caves for a hundred years | They were found on an island | They were never in danger
exercise: Is the eighth zodiac animal a goat or a sheep? | Both, the Chinese word can mean either | Only a goat with horns | Only a sheep | Neither, it is a deer from the forest
exercise: What wild bird did chickens come from? | The red junglefowl | The pheasant of Europe | The wild turkey of America | The peacock of India
exercise: Which animals were the first ever tamed by people? | Dogs | Pet cats | Horses | Chickens
exercise: Which wolves are thought to have become the first dogs? | Ones that were less afraid of people | The biggest and fiercest ones | Ones that lived in the sea | Ones that could not hunt
test: «rat won the race cos it's fastest, obviously» What does the legend say, and what is true? | It rode on the ox and jumped off; it is only a story | Rats really are faster than oxen and tigers over a long race | The rat really won a race in China | The rat flew over the river
test: Count them: a poster corner shows the rabbit, the horse and the dog. How many ears? | Six | Three | Eight | Twelve
test: Count them: the ox, the goat and the rooster stand in a row. How many legs? | Ten | Twelve | Eight | Six
test: What would happen if a horse pulled a plough through a deep flooded rice field instead of a buffalo? | It would sink deeper in the mud and tire faster | It would glide easily over the top of the mud | It would go faster than the buffalo | Nothing, horses and buffaloes are the same for this job
test: In Kung Fu Panda, Master Tigress is a strong fighter. A friend says tigers really have the word king written on their heads. What is right? | The stripes only look a bit like the character; they are camouflage | Yes, every tiger has a real letter written in black on its forehead | Only Chinese tigers can read | Tigers paint the mark on themselves
test: «grandma says theres a rabbit living on the moon, true??» What is right? | No living rabbit; it is a shape we see in old lava plains | Yes, astronauts saw it hopping about | Yes, Yutu is a real rabbit up there, it was sent with the rover | No, it is a cat
test: «buy the goat toy or the sheep toy for my zodiac year, which one's right, ffs» | Either, the Chinese word covers both | Only the goat is right | Only the sheep is right | Neither, it is a horse
test: «monkey at the temple looks hungry, gimme the crisps I'll feed him» What is the right answer? | No, never feed or tease wild monkeys; they grab and can bite | Yes, monkeys love crisps and are safe | Yes, if you hold the crisps up high | Only feed him with your hand held open flat, like you feed a horse
test: In Tom and Jerry the mouse always outsmarts the cat. Which real fact about rats and mice is closest? | They learn fast and are clever at finding food | They are stronger than cats | They can carry heavy cats | They never run from cats, they always stand and fight back
test: «zoo horses are just pets, they don't matter» Why are zoo animals sometimes important? | Przewalski's horses survived only in zoos and went back to the wild | Zoo horses can never go back to the wild, because they forget how to run | Zoos only keep cartoon animals | Wild horses are everywhere anyway
test: Why might a wolf that was less shy do better near a human camp long ago? | It could eat scraps and stay safe near people | It could hunt people more easily | It could steal the people's clothes | People fed only shy wolves
exam: State the cardinality of the set of real, living kinds of animal represented in the Chinese zodiac. | Eleven | Twelve | Ten | Nine
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the water buffalo was employed in the cultivation of flooded rice fields. | Its broad hooves distribute its weight, preventing deep sinking in mud | It perspires copiously and so remains cool in the sun | It is lighter than any horse | It is capable of swimming beneath the water
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Przewalski's horse. | It became extinct in the wild and was restored from animals kept in zoos | It has never been in any danger of extinction | It is a breed developed recently on the farms of Mongolia for racing | It survives only in legends of the zodiac
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origin of the domestic chicken? | It is descended from the red junglefowl of southern China and South-East Asia | It is descended from the European pheasant | It was first bred from the peacock in India | It is descended from the wild turkey of America
